addkeysection "RS HardPoints" RSHardPoints

// The engine redirects grip to these keys, per hand, ONLY when the grip
// arbiter resolves a clean tap (pressed and released, no other button joined
// it) that started inside a hardpoint's radius (see DoomXR
// vk_openxrdevice.cpp, HolsterClaim*/xrGripContext). Outside one, or during a
// combo, grip keeps its normal meaning entirely -- these never fire. That is
// why binding grip's holster role here does not steal grip from two-hand
// stabilize or the secondary-button modifier layer.
//
// NO DEFAULT BINDS -- bind these yourself in Customize Controls. The engine
// synthesises F13 (main) / F14 (off) for a holster-context grip, so those are
// the keys to bind if you want grip to work.
//
// THE ALIAS NAME IS PRIVATE, THE NETEVENT IS SHARED, and that split is what
// lets this pk3 load alongside RS_Holsters. A key binds to exactly ONE alias,
// so if both mods bound F13 to their own alias the second one loaded would
// silently win and the other would never see a grip press again. Both bind
// their own alias (no KEYCONF collision) and both fire rs-vrhp-grab-*, which
// every handler receives, and each mod's doSwap returns immediately unless
// the hand is inside one of ITS anchors.
//
// THAT IS NOT ARBITRATION, and this comment used to say it was ("a hand is
// only ever in one place"). It is false as soon as two anchors from the two
// mods overlap, which is easy -- a wrist mount rides the forearm and a hip
// holster sits on the body, and one reach can put the hand inside both. Both
// handlers receive the same netevent, both find a claim, and BOTH act on one
// press. A real grip arbiter is in progress; until it lands, one-mod-acts is
// a property of where the anchors happen to be, not of this design.
addmenukey "Hardpoint: store/draw (MAIN hand)" rs_hardpoint_main
alias rs_hardpoint_main "netevent rs-vrhp-grab-main"

addmenukey "Hardpoint: store/draw (OFF hand)" rs_hardpoint_off
alias rs_hardpoint_off "netevent rs-vrhp-grab-off"

// Re-takes the standing eye-height sample (RS_HardPoints.zs,
// ForceRecalibrate). ADDED 2026-08-26: NetworkProcess has handled
// "rs-hardpoint-recalibrate" since the split, but nothing anywhere fired it
// -- no alias here, no menu row -- so the only way to reach it was typing
// the netevent into the console by hand. It matters less here than in
// RS_Holsters (the arm mounts hang off a tracked controller, not off a body
// measured against eye height) but it is not dead weight either: eyeHeight
// is what anchorPos scales edFrac by, and the whole system refuses to run at
// all until calibration lands, so "sat down during the auto sample" is still
// a state you need a way out of.
addmenukey "Hardpoint: recalibrate height" rs_hardpoint_recal
alias rs_hardpoint_recal "netevent rs-hardpoint-recalibrate"

// Dumps head pose, both hand positions, every anchor's world position and
// distance from each hand, and (if anything is stored) the exact orientation/
// offset each item's model measured plus what got applied. This is the tool
// for "nothing is happening" or "it's still wrong" -- it turns a silent
// non-event, or an unexplained visual bug, into numbers.
addmenukey "Hardpoint: debug dump" rs_hardpoint_debug
alias rs_hardpoint_debug "netevent rs-hardpoint-debug"

// Edit mode: drag the hardpoint spheres onto your actual arm, then dump the
// numbers. Beats deriving offsets from proportion tables and hoping.
addmenukey "Hardpoint: EDIT MODE toggle" rs_hardpoint_edit
alias rs_hardpoint_edit "netevent rs-hardpoint-edit"

addmenukey "Hardpoint: print tuned table" rs_hardpoint_table
alias rs_hardpoint_table "netevent rs-hardpoint-table"

addmenukey "Hardpoint: reset offsets" rs_hardpoint_reset
alias rs_hardpoint_reset "netevent rs-hardpoint-reset"

// The tuned layout, saved to disk. ONE slot, not the seated/standing pair
// RS_Holsters has -- those mounts hang off a body, where a table tuned
// standing does not fit a seated one; these are bolted to a tracked
// controller, so posture is not a variable and two slots would be two names
// for the same numbers. One slot still covers BOTH arms: each arm has its
// own layout (it has to -- the mounts sit relative to whichever controller
// wears them) but the two occupy separate key ranges in the same file, so
// one save writes both and one load restores both. No default binds:
// setup-time actions, not something you want a stray controller press
// triggering mid-game.
addmenukey "Hardpoint: save tuned layout" rs_hardpoint_save_layout
alias rs_hardpoint_save_layout "netevent rs-hardpoint-save-layout"

addmenukey "Hardpoint: load tuned layout" rs_hardpoint_load_layout
alias rs_hardpoint_load_layout "netevent rs-hardpoint-load-layout"

// GESTURE-CAST fire. WRISTS FIRE, HOLSTERS DRAW: these do NOT draw the
// weapon. The mount's weapon runs its own Fire state in place, invisibly,
// and goes straight back on the mount -- see RS_HardPoints.zs' fireGesture.
// Drawing is the torso holsters' verb (RS_Holsters), deliberately.
//
// SIX ALIASES, three per arm, because an arm arms its OWN three mounts: roll
// that wrist palm-out and its trio becomes live. That is the mirror of
// store/draw, where a hand can only reach the OTHER arm's bank. Each alias
// is hardcoded to ONE mount index, keeping the per-button assignment the
// owner made on 2026-08-25: grip -> Below, pad/X -> Knuckle,
// trigger -> Joint.
//
// THE OFF TRIO KEPT ITS ORIGINAL ALIAS NAMES, NETEVENT NAMES AND INDICES
// (3/4/5) through the 2026-08-26 re-layout, so any bind a player already
// made still means exactly what it meant. The main trio is new and takes
// 0/1/2, which is where the deferred forearm row used to sit.
//
// Only takes effect while THAT hand is rolled into the arming pose
// (RS_HardPoints.zs, updateGestureArm/updateGestureArmMain) -- pressing
// these outside that pose does nothing, on purpose.
//
// PLACEHOLDER BINDS -- bind to a throwaway test key for now, NOT to the
// real grip/trigger/pad. Wiring these onto the actual controller buttons
// safely needs engine-side context gating that does not exist yet: without
// it, a raw trigger read would ALSO fire whatever that hand's own held
// weapon does on that same physical pull. Once that engine work lands,
// rebind onto whatever gated key it publishes.
addmenukey "Hardpoint: OFF gesture-fire GRIP (mount 3)" rs_hardpoint_gesture_grip
alias rs_hardpoint_gesture_grip "netevent rs-hardpoint-gesture-grip"

addmenukey "Hardpoint: OFF gesture-fire PAD/X (mount 4)" rs_hardpoint_gesture_padx
alias rs_hardpoint_gesture_padx "netevent rs-hardpoint-gesture-padx"

addmenukey "Hardpoint: OFF gesture-fire TRIGGER (mount 5)" rs_hardpoint_gesture_trigger
alias rs_hardpoint_gesture_trigger "netevent rs-hardpoint-gesture-trigger"

addmenukey "Hardpoint: MAIN gesture-fire GRIP (mount 0)" rs_hardpoint_gesture_main_grip
alias rs_hardpoint_gesture_main_grip "netevent rs-hardpoint-gesture-main-grip"

addmenukey "Hardpoint: MAIN gesture-fire PAD/X (mount 1)" rs_hardpoint_gesture_main_padx
alias rs_hardpoint_gesture_main_padx "netevent rs-hardpoint-gesture-main-padx"

addmenukey "Hardpoint: MAIN gesture-fire TRIGGER (mount 2)" rs_hardpoint_gesture_main_trigger
alias rs_hardpoint_gesture_main_trigger "netevent rs-hardpoint-gesture-main-trigger"
